WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.090
Inside a chloroplast, most
electrons flow one way:

2
00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.950
from water, through photosystem
two, then photosystem one.

3
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:13.240
Along that path, water is
split and oxygen is released.

4
00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.690
But some electrons take a detour.

5
00:00:15.740 --> 00:00:18.840
They leave photosystem one,
pass along the carriers,

6
00:00:18.890 --> 00:00:20.520
and come straight back.

7
00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.000
No water is split.

8
00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:23.920
No oxygen is made.

9
00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:28.480
So why would a chloroplast send its
electrons in a loop that makes no oxygen at

10
00:00:28.530 --> 00:00:29.420
all?

11
00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:34.100
In this mission, you'll see how hundreds
of pigments funnel energy to one reaction

12
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.180
centre.

13
00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:40.740
Then you'll revisit a 1966
experiment: thylakoids soaked in acid,

14
00:00:40.790 --> 00:00:42.580
in total darkness.

15
00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:47.880
Your mission: explain why a chloroplast
would run a loop that makes no oxygen.
